-- rules/worklist.rofl — THE PLAN, DERIVED FROM WHAT THE MODEL KNOWS ABOUT ITSELF
--
-- The queue is not a list somebody keeps. `rules/js-model.rofl` already answers
-- which cells are open and whose fault each unknown is; a plan adds exactly
-- three things — ORDER, STATE, and the link from an item to the problems it
-- spawned — and those are the only things authored here. Everything else is
-- derived, so the plan cannot drift from the model: closing a cell moves the
-- queue with no edit, and an item claiming a cell the model says is shut is red.
--
-- WHY THE CLAIM IS `claim(queued, ...)` AND NOT A RELATION OF ITS OWN.
-- CLAUDE.md records the incident by name: `orphan_claim[audit]` was written by
-- hand for `handled` and `ignored`, and ONE new authored relation over the same
-- arguments silently reopened the whole vocabulary hole. The remedy landed
-- afterwards — `claim/6` is extensional and `orphan[audit]` audits every ledger
-- at once — so a fourth ledger costs ONE fact, `claim_kind(queued)`, and
-- INHERITS the check instead of copying it. A queue item pointing at a kind,
-- shape or layer nobody declares is caught by a rule written before this file
-- existed. Measured rather than assumed: test/worklist.test.ts plants one.
--
-- THE GRANULARITY IS THE MODEL'S FINEST, NOT A NEW ONE. `cell[audit]/4` carries
-- the shape column, with `none` for a kind the axis does not split, so the queue
-- is keyed at shape level where a layer has shapes and at kind level where it
-- does not — without this file deciding anything about axes.
--
-- WHAT THIS LEDGER MAY NOT DO: tick a cell. `verdict[audit]` reads `handled`
-- and `ignored` only, so QUEUEING WORK IS NOT DOING IT, by construction.

edb(work).          -- work(Item, Note)             an item, and what it is
edb(work_order).    -- work_order(Item, N)          the order to take them in
edb(work_state).    -- work_state(Item, State)      open | taken | done
edb(work_sweeps).   -- work_sweeps(Item, Layer)     owns the layer's residue
edb(work_spawned).  -- work_spawned(Item, Finding)  a problem this item found
edb(work_state_kind).

work_state_kind(open).
work_state_kind(taken).
work_state_kind(done).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE OPEN QUEUE, DERIVED. An IRREDUCIBLE unknown is not work: `import(pathVar)`
-- names a module that does not exist until the expression runs, and no amount
-- of modelling recovers it. `our_unknown` is exactly the half that is ours.
open_cell[audit](K, S, L) :- our_unknown[audit](js, K, S, L).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- CLAIMED, SWEPT, AND THE THREE WAYS A PLAN CAN LIE
--
-- A cell is claimed by name, or swept in bulk by an item that owns whatever the
-- named items leave behind in its layer. The bucket is EXPLICIT rather than
-- implied: a layer with no sweeping item reports its residue as unqueued, which
-- is the honest reading of `nobody has said who does these`.
claimed(K, S, L) :- claim(queued, js, K, S, L, _).
-- a DONE sweeper stops sweeping, so a bucket marked finished with cells still
-- open reports them as unqueued again. That is wider than `false_done`, which
-- can only see cells claimed by name.
sweeper(K, S, L) :- work_sweeps(W, L), not work_state(W, done),
                    open_cell[audit](K, S, L), not claimed(K, S, L).

-- LIE 1: an open cell nobody has queued — the plan is silent about real work.
unqueued[audit](K, S, L) :- open_cell[audit](K, S, L), not claimed(K, S, L),
                            not sweeper(K, S, L).

-- LIE 2: an item claiming a cell the model does not report as open, either
-- because somebody else closed it or because it was never open. A DONE item is
-- exempt: closing the cell is what done MEANS.
-- THE SHAPE COLUMN IS IN THE HEAD ON PURPOSE. The first draft projected it
-- away, and eleven distinct shapes of one kind collapsed into a single row —
-- an audit that under-reports by exactly the amount of refinement the model
-- has. Caught by a mutant that expected eleven rows and got one.
queue_stale[audit](W, K, S, L) :- claim(queued, js, K, S, L, W), not work_state(W, done),
                                  not open_cell[audit](K, S, L).

-- LIE 3: two items owning one cell. Not caught by anything above — measured as
-- a surviving mutant before this rule was written — and it matters because the
-- queue is what says who is doing what: two owners is nobody.
double_owned[audit](K, S, L, A, B) :- claim(queued, js, K, S, L, A),
                                      claim(queued, js, K, S, L, B), A != B.

-- LIE 4, AND IT IS THE ONE docs/modelling-a-language.md FEARS BY NAME — a
-- filled matrix looks finished. An item marked done whose cell the model still
-- reports open is a claim of completion the model itself contradicts.
false_done[audit](W, K, S, L) :- work_state(W, done), claim(queued, js, K, S, L, W),
                                 open_cell[audit](K, S, L).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHERE THIS PACK CANNOT LOOK, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER.
--
-- A SWEEP ABSORBS A LOST CLAIM. Measured as a surviving mutant: deleting
-- `claim(queued, js, new_expression, s_new_result, callgraph, ...)` outright
-- moved NO audit, because the layer's sweeping item immediately owns whatever
-- is unclaimed. That is what a bucket is FOR, so it is not a defect in the
-- rule — it is a defect in what the rule can see, and no rule here can close it
-- without history the store does not keep.
--
-- The detector is therefore a NUMBER, and it lives in test/worklist.test.ts:
-- how many cells each sweep absorbs is pinned, so specificity leaking into a
-- bucket moves a figure somebody has to re-state on purpose. `sweeper/3` is
-- exported for exactly that and for no other reason.

-- ordinary hygiene of an authored table, each direction a positive row
ordered(W)                    :- work_order(W, _).
stated_state(W)               :- work_state(W, _).
work_unstated[audit](W)       :- claim(queued, js, _, _, _, W), not work(W, _).
work_unstated[audit](W)       :- work_sweeps(W, _), not work(W, _).
work_unordered[audit](W)      :- work(W, _), not ordered(W).
work_stateless[audit](W)      :- work(W, _), not stated_state(W).
work_bad_state[audit](W, S)   :- work_state(W, S), not work_state_kind(S).
work_sweeps_nolayer[audit](W, L) :- work_sweeps(W, L), not layer(L).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT TO DO NEXT, as a query rather than as a habit
earlier_open(N)     :- work_state(W, open), work_order(W, M), M < N.
next_work[audit](W) :- work_state(W, open), work_order(W, N), not earlier_open(N).
in_flight[audit](W) :- work_state(W, taken).

-- WHAT AN ITEM HAS COST SO FAR, in problems found. The plan and the ledger are
-- separate files and this rule is the only thing joining them, so it fires only
-- in a world holding both — which is why the test loads both rather than one.
spawned_by[audit](W, F)   :- work_spawned(W, F), finding(F, _).
spawn_orphan[audit](W, F) :- work_spawned(W, F), not finding(F, _).
